# Requests for the .http client: one per `###` block, run from the block the caret is in.
# Every address is under example.com (RFC 2606), so nothing here reaches a real service.

### List orders
GET https://api.example.com/v1/orders?status=open&limit=20
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer example-not-a-real-token

### Create an order
POST https://api.example.com/v1/orders
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "customer": "Acme Widgets",
  "items": [
    { "sku": "WID-001", "quantity": 3 },
    { "sku": "WID-042", "quantity": 1 }
  ],
  "note": "Leave at reception; ring twice"
}

### Update a shipping address (a form body)
PUT https://api.example.com/v1/orders/1042/address
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

line1=12+Example+Street&city=Springfield&postcode=EX1+2MP

### Delete a draft, with a header value that holds a colon
DELETE https://api.example.com/v1/drafts/77
If-Match: "v3:2026-10-03T09:00:00Z"

### A lowercase method and a port
get http://localhost:8080/health

### HTTP version on the request line (the form most editors write)
GET https://api.example.com/v1/status HTTP/1.1
Accept: text/plain

### A relative address: no scheme, so the client offers nothing to run
GET /v1/orders

### Comments only: nothing to run
# A block can hold notes and no request at all.
